WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.540
Michael Berridge worked on blowfly salivary glands.

2
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:12.760
He showed that IP3 appears within seconds of the hormone arriving.

3
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.560
Eight years earlier, Robert Michell had proposed that breaking down inositol

4
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.360
lipids is how receptors raise calcium.

5
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.700
But a hormone starts many things at once.

6
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.800
Did IP3 raise the calcium, or did some third event?

7
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.760
To test it, you need IP3 inside a cell with no hormone outside.

8
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.960
But IP3 is too charged to cross a membrane.

9
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.300
So the cell has to be opened.

10
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:49.720
In Irene Schulz's laboratory in Frankfurt, H. Streb permeabilized pancreatic acinar

11
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.920
cells. That makes the plasma membrane leaky to small molecules, while the

12
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.820
compartments inside stay sealed.

13
00:00:57.683 --> 00:01:03.723
Robin Irvine had prepared IP3 in England, from human red blood cells.

14
00:01:04.503 --> 00:01:06.223
It was added to the opened cells.

15
00:01:07.043 --> 00:01:08.483
No hormone was added.

16
00:01:09.463 --> 00:01:14.523
Calcium came out of an internal store, and that store was not the mitochondria.

17
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:19.093
So IP3 was sufficient without a hormone.

18
00:01:19.973 --> 00:01:25.393
Later work identified the store as the endoplasmic reticulum, and the target as the

19
00:01:25.393 --> 00:01:32.273
IP3 receptor. This channel is built of four subunits, and it opens when IP3 binds.

20
00:01:33.213 --> 00:01:38.533
The store holds free calcium at a thousand times the resting level or more, so an

21
00:01:38.533 --> 00:01:40.533
open channel means a fast rise.
